 Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T07:24:07-07:00","id":1450170,"title":"","content":"Finsihed reading it and loved it from cover to cover. The rule sections kind of lost me at times, but that's mainly because I don't really know them by heart in the core game and certainly not in English, which isn't my first language <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> !<br /> <br /> Still, an excellent work, and I'm happy to have started with it, even if I'm now afraid that your older two will seems too short XD !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","name":"Ur-Than","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T17:38:35-07:00","id":1450240,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ur-Than</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450170#post1450170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Finsihed reading it and loved it from cover to cover. The rule sections kind of lost me at times, but that's mainly because I don't really know them by heart in the core game and certainly not in English, which isn't my first language <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> !<br /> <br /> Still, an excellent work, and I'm happy to have started with it, even if I'm now afraid that your older two will seems too short XD !</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm so glad you enjoyed it! We've had some preliminary discussions about expanding <b>Strange Shades </b>(a couple more Horrors, another ghoul family, etc.), but that'd be a little ways down the road.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T19:38:35-07:00","id":1450389,"title":"","content":"Hey all, as scheduled, tomorrow I'll have an excerpt, but I thought I might slip in the playlist I made for the book, if you're into that sort of thing. Unlike the Nosferatu one I made, this isn't really keyed to any bloodlines (though there's some Malkavian inspiration here too.) This was inspirational music as I was doing layout and development, as well as some other Ventrue-oriented tracks I've come across.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/playlist/1YIjmQ7v5k237yrCjewm12?si=ca8c0b840c1545d3\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/playlist/1YIjmQ7v5k237yrCjewm12?si=ca8c0b840c1545d3\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/playlist/1YIjmQ7v5k237yrCjewm12?si=ca8c0b840c1545d3\" target=\"_blank\"> https://open.spotify.com/playlist/1YIjmQ7v5k237yrCjewm12?si=ca8c0b840c1545d3 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T11:57:51-07:00","id":1450475,"title":"","content":"Happy Monday everyone! Time for an excerpt.<br /> <br /> So last time I said I'd do the Warumono, but that's because I'm a dope and forgot the Typhos. So, we're talking about them today, because they have a much more interesting development story to riff on.<br /> <br /> The Typhos are the bloodline who had the most ink spilled on them over the course of the project. In first drafts, they still had the medical aesthetic and &quot;fake pharma company&quot; front, but otherwise their goals were very different. Initially, they were more trans-vampirist; instead of trying to achieve more metaphysical changes like the Dragons, they focused on physically perfecting bodies. It was a little old school Tzimisce, though without the spiritual aspects.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, this concept didn't really work. While the aesthetics were different, they were just Ordo Dracul with a twist. That said, I liked the bloodline of Ventrue surgeons concept; power takes many forms, and a physician has immense power over his patients. With that in mind, we retooled the idea to be more external. Instead of themselves, the Typhos would work on perfecting their victims \u2014 perfect, docile prey who can never fight back. This concept lent itself better to a horror theme, and made the bloodline more useful as antagonists. We more or less write these bloodlines from the perspective that players will use them as characters, but we always make sure they have utility for Storytellers.<br /> <br /> The other thing thing that needed reworking was their bloodline gift. Initially this was a form of Blood Sorcery with a fleshly and medical overtones. While it wasn't a bad idea in the abstract, it ultimately ended up being a fancy way of doing Protean. I also felt like we'd done a fair number of blood sorcery variants in these books, and I wanted to break out of that format. But if this wasn't going to be blood sorcery, how was it going to work? Devotions didn't really interest either of us, as that seemed a bit boring, and not fitting to the theme. We also considered Scales of the Dragon, but A) we didn't really want to give them a Coil (them being too much like the Ordo being the problem in the first place) and B) we already basically did that with the Adrestoi. I had initially suggested the procedures be Conditions you could put on other characters, but the author pointed out that this meant that the player character doesn't really get much out of that, particularly considering the bloodline's themes. That got us thinking, though: what if these are asymmetrical powers? You benefit from one effect, the other character benefits from another. And which system in the core book already does that? Invictus Oaths.<br /> <br /> Using Oaths as the basis for this system (Arthmoic Surgery) is one of my favourite little mechanical flourishes in the book. It makes what the Typhos do just as much a social as a medical thing, and it does away with needing to do a lot of rolling. I figure, if they've let you be a Typhos, you probably don't have much trouble performing surgery. That, and using the Oaths system suggests there's more to what the bloodline is doing than just surgery.<br /> <br /> So, enjoy the icky machinations of the Typhos Institute. Tomorrow I'll be back with the Warumono, for real this time.<br /> <br /> <b>Typhos: The ones who make you better</b><br /> <br /> <i>\u201cSide effects? Those were outlined in the waiver. You did read it, didn\u2019t you?\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The clinic is spotless, all shiny glass and gleaming tile. The magazines in their little stand are immaculate, like they\u2019ve never even been read. Is this your second time here or your third? Has it been more than three? You know that answer should be important, but it\u2019s not \u2014 not now, anyway. The only important thing is getting more. More of those little red pills, more of the feeling they gave you. Every time you\u2019re here (and it\u2019s definitely been more than three) you come away a little\u2026 <i>less</i>, but the doctor says he can help with that, too. There\u2019s a buzz from the receptionist\u2019s desk, and her gleaming smile mirrors your own desperate one. The doctor is in.<br /> <br /> Kindred are parasites. For all their pretension and personal power, they\u2019re utterly dependent on the teeming host of humanity, and should that willfully ignorant mass ever decide vampires are real, they would be consumed in the resulting conflagration. Herbert Spencer Cartwright has spent his Requiem grappling with this problem, and in the Typhos Institute, he believes he\u2019s found a solution: The most successful parasites are the ones that alter their host to suit their needs.<br /> <br /> The Typhos Institute is an international biomedical research group, privately owned and controlled by Cartwright\u2019s childer and grandchilder, and employing hundreds of ghouls and mortals. Never publicly traded, the Institute is insular and secretive, its human experiments unknown to all but the Surgeons themselves, and perhaps a few trusted (and blood-bound) employees. The Institute runs several for-profit clinics in the UK, the Americas, and parts of Asia, where it cultivates a list of loyal clients who double as the bloodline\u2019s test subjects. Cartwright himself is still active, and still working toward the goal that inspired him to found his bloodline in the first place: \u2014 creating a better human\u2026 to rule.<br /> <br /> The Typhos work to engineer a new breed of mortal, docile, unquestioning, and content in their role as the Kindred\u2019s food source. These efforts have yet to produce satisfactory results, but the techniques they\u2019ve pioneered over the centuries have been refined into Arthmoic Surgery, a collection of insidious procedures capable of enhancing their subjects physically, but also of making them more susceptible the predations of vampires.<br /> <br /> Typhos exploit their patients by preying on their fear of mortality, claiming their procedures can extend lifespans, improve health, and cure illnesses\u2026 for a nominal fee. Unlike many other luxury clinics looking to make money off the insecure rich, the Surgeons\u2019 methods actually work, but they also extract a higher price. Arthmoic Surgery makes you better, but what it mostly makes you better at is being a victim. Patients find themselves losing time, having difficulty thinking critically, and being very willing to sign up for whatever new procedure their predatory clinician prescribes. All of which would be alarming for the patient, if they still had the capacity or will to question their own actions.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s all relatively small-scale for now. The Typhos only have so many members capable of performing Arthmoic Surgery, and the exclusivity of their clinics means their victims/patients\u2019 number in the high hundreds, at best. Cartwright is always looking to the future, however, and with technology like cloning and gene therapy on the horizon, his ambitions to \u201cimprove\u201d humanity are global.<br /> <br /> Other Kindred look upon the Sawbones with suspicion, saying they operate too openly and that their ambitions threaten the Masquerade. However, few can deny their effectiveness, as their methods for creating obedient blood dolls are more than proven. The fact that they take commissions, and will apply their techniques to any mortal of a vampire\u2019s choosing (no questions asked) also has a lot to do with the All Night Society\u2019s continued tolerance of their work. They\u2019re even willing to give a down-on-their-luck Lick a break on their usual prices, so long as the buyer doesn\u2019t mind being a test subject himself, or making a few bodily donations. All in the name of science, of course.<br /> <br /> <b>Why you want to be us</b><br /> <br /> You believe in a better future for all Kindred, one in which humans no longer fight their place in the natural order. You tire of the nightly hunt, and wish to learn how to make your food come to you. You enjoy opening people up to see what makes them tick.<br /> <br /> <b>Why you should fear us</b><br /> <br /> It\u2019s not us you should fear. Our patients are many, powerful, and ever so loyal. Our work is for the good of all, but we won\u2019t hesitate to remove any who threaten it. Do not stand in the way of progress, lest you be trampled by it.<br /> <br /> <b>Why we should fear ourselves</b><br /> <br /> What if, in trying to adapt the host to our needs, it instead notices us and crushes us before we\u2019re ready? It\u2019s too late to stop now; we\u2019re as addicted to control as our patients are to being controlled, and we won\u2019t stop until we\u2019ve perfected it \u2014 or it kills us.<br /> <br /> <b>Twists of the Blood: Conspiracy Theory</b><br /> <br /> Readers with access to Deviant: The Renegades may note the similarities between the Typhos Institute and the grasping, shadowy conspiracies that hunt the Remade. While this isn\u2019t their primary purpose, the Institute\u2019s goal of creating a \u201cbetter\u201d human could easily result in Divergence. Alternatively, if the Typhos discovered the existence of Deviants, they might try and capture one for study and dissection. After all, what could be more terrifying than discovering that the corrupt pharmaceutical company hunting you down is run by <i>literal </i>vampires.<br /> <br /> Used as a conspiracy in the Web of Pain, the Typhos Institute has a Standing of 3\u20135; Nodes are primarily found throughout the United Kingdom, India, and North America; Icons relate to extreme medical practices; and any individual Surgeon can be a Linchpin character. See p. 217 of Deviant: The Renegades for more on conspiracies and their plots.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-01-2021, 04:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T13:58:05-07:00","id":1450498,"title":"","content":"The part about Deviants in the end makes me ask myself how might the Institute interact or clash with the Cheiron Group...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T14:10:45-07:00","id":1450502,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450498#post1450498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The part about Deviants in the end makes me ask myself how might the Institute interact or clash with the Cheiron Group...</div> </div> </div> </div>Clash is definitely the operative word...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>False Gods: Ventrue, p. 57</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u201cDo you really think they can just turn people into meat puppets, and nobody is going to notice? Oh, the kine know, they absolutely know, but they\u2019re not shutting it down. Hell no, they\u2019re trying to figure out how the creepy scalpel-lickers do it! Corporations, deep government, marketing execs \u2014 they\u2019d all love to know how to create a better brand of sheeple.\u201d<br /> <br /> Half-true, in that the Typhos Institute has drawn some human attention, but not the passive kind. The Cheiron Group has long been a collector of Kindred, harvesting Vitae to use in their own grotesque surgeries. However, the international conglomerate has taken a different tactic with the Surgeons: scorched earth. Whenever possible, Cheiron hunters seize and destroy Typhos assets, and burn the Sawbones themselves to ashes. The Institute doesn\u2019t know what they\u2019ve done to earn the wrath of Cheiron, except perhaps for Cartwright himself. Some Surgeons take the antagonism of such a powerful group as proof their research is headed in the right direction, and news of a Cheiron attack can imbue them with a work ethic bordering on zealotry.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T14:17:58-07:00","id":1450503,"title":"","content":"First, I want to say that it was great getting to do some of the art for this! In particular, I think I really hit my stride with the ghouls. Thanks so much for letting me contribute!<br /> <br /> Next, there is a lot to love in the book's content. The bloodlines are all interesting in one way or another, and I definitely found myself wishing I had the Keravnos back when I was running my Promethean game. Especially with how Powdermen might look like Pandorans if a Created squints. Heck! It could be fun to put a Keravnos into a throng of Prometheans because of that. I also thought that it was really inspired to base the Arthmoic Surgery of the Typhos on Invictus Oaths. Finally, I'm always a slut for the Rotgrafen, and this version does not disappoint. Love their style merit and <i>especially</i> their Bane. Far more Lordly.<br /> <br /> The Malkavia chronicle is very thoughtfully presented, and I like this take that leans more into the oracular aspects of it over the &quot;lol insane&quot; bits. I've never found myself much a fan of Malkavia or Dementation, but tying it to Dominate is very cool, and [imo] better fits the Ventrue theme of control. I think my favorite part of the section is the Malkovian update at the end. I like how Pareidolia is uncomplicated and gets to the heart of things.<br /> <br /> The biggest surprise for me was how the advice on handling the Aloof Curse is probably my favorite part of the book?? It can be a little difficult to understand the Aloof Curse, and the section makes for a useful guide. The way it describes the loss of Touchstones as a slow build-up of excuses and missed opportunities makes the Ventrue experience a lot more relatable, too. Also, that Merit section is full of some absolutely <i>wild</i> stuff I could see entire character concepts built around. Extremely potent, and occasionally extremely funky. Looking at you Best Fiend, Eternal Predator, Owl-Blooded, and Proclamation.<br /> <br /> The Architects of the Monolith are possibly my first pick for a Covenant next time I play Vampire. I just love their vibes and Gilded Cage is very fun. Also, love that splat art, it has so much character! The Faithful are very fun, too. I like seeing how weird vampire stuff can get, even if I tend to run things a little vanilla in my games [I swear, if I was only allowed to run games with a single book for the rest of my life, it might be the <b>VtR2e</b> core].<br /> <br /> I think this is easily my favorite of the Clanbook updates so far! Good work to everyone involved!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450240#post1450240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm so glad you enjoyed it! We've had some preliminary discussions about expanding <b>Strange Shades </b>(a couple more Horrors, another ghoul family, etc.), but that'd be a little ways down the road.</div> </div> </div> </div>*flashback to the Mantis Clan DLC*<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Posting gives me anxiety, but I'm still happy to be here</i><br /> Habitual Lurker, Vengrel Liker, Acolyte Apologist, She/Her","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25955-somethingfishy","name":"SomethingFishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25955_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T14:28:14-07:00","id":1450506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450502#post1450502\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Clash is definitely the operative word...<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>My head canon is that Cheiron hunts the Typhos because they hate to competition- I always treated Cheiron &quot;endgame&quot; as trying to create the &quot;perfect human/hunter&quot; out of monster parts, and they slowly work towards it through their countless incarnations. Then, the Typhos come (perhaps by stealing some trade secrets from the company) and mess up their plan by creating their own brand of &quot;perfected humans&quot; in a way which messes up Cheiron's endgame, which means that they must be burned down.<br /> <br /> Just how I incorporate this material into my personal CofDverse :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T15:24:13-07:00","id":1450513,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SomethingFishy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450503#post1450503\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, I want to say that it was great getting to do some of the art for this! In particular, I think I really hit my stride with the ghouls. Thanks so much for letting me contribute!</div> </div> </div> </div>I was so glad to have you onboard! Fish did the illustrations for the ghoul families and two of the Malkavians, and really knocked those pieces out of the park! Everyone should go check out her website!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Malkavia chronicle is very thoughtfully presented, and I like this take that leans more into the oracular aspects of it over the &quot;lol insane&quot; bits. I've never found myself much a fan of Malkavia or Dementation, but tying it to Dominate is very cool, and [imo] better fits the Ventrue theme of control. I think my favorite part of the section is the Malkovian update at the end. I like how Pareidolia is uncomplicated and gets to the heart of things. </div> </div> </div>I was very happy with the way the Malk<u>o</u>vians turned out; I allllmost didn't bother with them, but I'm glad I did in the end. They're pretty much exactly how I'd run a more straightforward Second Edition version of Malkavia. And that Pareidolia Condition is very emblematic of my design philosophy: simple, powerful, and painful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The biggest surprise for me was how the advice on handling the Aloof Curse is probably my favorite part of the book?? It can be a little difficult to understand the Aloof Curse, and the section makes for a useful guide. The way it describes the loss of Touchstones as a slow build-up of excuses and missed opportunities makes the Ventrue experience a lot more relatable, too. Also, that Merit section is full of some absolutely <i>wild</i> stuff I could see entire character concepts built around. Extremely potent, and occasionally extremely funky. Looking at you Best Fiend, Eternal Predator, Owl-Blooded, and Proclamation. </div> </div> </div>Getting that Aloof Curse essay in the book was <i>very </i>important to me, and I think it's the best thing in chapter three. The Ventrue easily have the bane that's the most difficult to wrap your head around in the abstract (though I think it bares its teeth more obviously in play). But it's also the bane that has some of the most interesting roleplaying opportunities; I just wanted to make sure people knew what they were. The author really hit the nail on the head by making it the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7kWPOa4Gy0\" target=\"_blank\">King of Empty Promises Curse</a>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Architects of the Monolith are possibly my first pick for a Covenant next time I play Vampire. I just love their vibes and Gilded Cage is very fun. Also, love that splat art, it has so much character! </div> </div> </div>I had a lot of fun writing them, and I'll have some fun dev notes very soon on how I came to make them a covenant. I also love that illustration. Fun fact: she's doing a similar pose to the first edition splat art from <b>Bloodlines: The Hidden</b>, which was <i>completely </i>unintentional on the artist's part. Just a fun coincidence (OR WAS IT THEIR PLAN ALL ALONG?)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T12:12:27-07:00","id":1450623,"title":"","content":"Alright, time to take a long look at the Warumono (not a difficult task, really).<br /> <br /> The Wild Ones are the fourth of our new bloodlines, and one of the things I like best about them is that they capture a bit of the gonzo charm of a &quot;First Edition\u2013style&quot; bloodline while still being a sleek Second Edition design. By which I mean, their niche powers (i.e., mysterious tattoos) are the kind of thing you could probably imagine finding in some lost <b>Bloodlines: The Whoever </b>book. They're also Japanese, a frequent White Wolf subject. It was also important to us to offer more non-North American/European character choices, but without falling into some of the stereotypes and insensitivities that make certain older bloodlines a little hard to read in 2021.<br /> <br /> The Warumono didn't change too much in development. I asked for a bit more high crime drama in second drafts, but otherwise the idea is unaltered. Most changes were mechanical. Originally, the tattoos were several separate Merits, but that was unwieldy for a number of reasons, so we condensed them into four Style Merits. That was a bit more thematic and aesthetically interesting, and not a nightmare for me to squash together in layout, I might add. And whereas they originally just gained Vigor, we also swapped out Animalism for Celerity \u2014 and thus the full suite of physical Disciplines \u2014 because why the hell not? Wild Ones gotta be wild. Their bane is pretty much the same conceptually, though I tweaked it a bit to refer explicitly to the lost visage, and made it more player-facing; originally it was a bonus to NPC actions to spot them, but I find banes work best when the player has a more immediate sense of when they go off. I'm also a fan of reducing Storyteller rolling.<br /> <br /> So, without further ado, here's the Warumono. Tomorrow I'll be including some dev notes on the Rotgrafen and Malocusians. After that, I <i>think </i>the Architects of the Monolith will be Thursday's topic; they've also been previewed on the forums, but their recreation as a covenant was kind of a story in itself.<br /> <br /> <b>Warumono: The ones who give you trouble</b><br /> <br /> <i>\u201cYou don\u2019t want a problem? Too bad. I\u2019m already here.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> You know they\u2019ve arrived before you see them. When you do, it\u2019s unmistakable. Loud suits, slicked hair, and an aura of menace rolling off them thicker than the scent of cigarettes and old cologne. Something inside you squirms and makes you search for an exit. The Warumono are here, and only danger follows on their snakeskin heels.<br /> <br /> The world is a machine that grinds down the weak and feeds on the suffering of the vulnerable. The poor, the outcasts, and the ostracized \u2014 for those on the bottom, the only way out is up. The Warumono bloodline epitomizes strength through perseverance, and the will to pull yourself up by blood-soaked bootstraps. The Wild Ones originated on the bottom rung of the All Night Society, but have grown into one of Japan\u2019s most powerful bloodlines. Their traditions of fraternity and courage have united them against their social betters, and as their influence grows, domains all across the world will soon tremble beneath the pristine boots of these immortal gangsters.<br /> <br /> The Warumono are a troublesome, but necessary counterbalance to the rigid hierarchies and social stratification of the Danse Macabre. The services they provide may exist outside the realms of haut monde, but they\u2019re always in demand. Their domain is the shadowy underbelly cast by the light of society, and within this darkness they thrive. The Warumono have no qualms about working with anyone, though they do have a soft spot for the underdog. And with is the key word. The Wild Ones work for themselves first and others second. Consider the leather jacket-clad knight in San Francisco as he runs protection for Invictus businesses; consider the shock troop as he fights alongside a pack of werewolves in Myanmar; consider the firebug as she burns down a prince\u2019s blood doll brothel. Each of them mercenary in their intentions, and each as untamable as a storm.<br /> <br /> As befits their sobriquet, the Wild Ones are anything but subtle. Each member is cursed to wear their power like a mantle, radiating outward and announcing their presence like a trumpet. They refer to it as the mark of the dragon, a sign of their rise against the natural order. Where they go, chaos follows. To help contain their overwhelming power, each member of the bloodline follows the philosophy of <i>gokudo</i>, the Extreme Path, a set of principles that governs their behavior and guides their unorthodox existences. Their devotion to this philosophy is so ardent that it\u2019s written into their flesh, through ornate tattoos that grant their owners supernatural powers beyond the ken of most other Kindred.<br /> <br /> The Warumono have a reputation as being aggressive, brash, potent defenders of the old and dark customs of their nation\u2019s criminal empires. Their progenitors were yakuza gangsters, gifted the Embrace in order to bolster Japanese Kindred against the influx of foreign vampires, and they soon grew to be an institution. The Wild Ones thrive even as the mortal yakuza dwindles in influence; the world always has room for the kind of violence and control they bring to bear. Whether roaming the countryside in biker gangs or stalking the back alleys of urban sprawls, the Warumono are animate typhoons whose passing brings peace.<br /> <br /> One way or another.<br /> <br /> <b>Why you want to be us</b><br /> <br /> You know there\u2019s a right way and a wrong way to do things \u2014 and then there\u2019s the way that actually gets it done. You honor what\u2019s come before because the past has value, but the path stretches out before you too, and you have to be a bad enough motherfucker to walk it. And who cares what anyone else says? If you\u2019re tough enough, who\u2019s going to argue with you? Or maybe you just want all the cool tattoos. Can\u2019t blame you there.<br /> <br /> <b>Why you should fear us</b><br /> <br /> Where we go, trouble follows. We wear the storm like a fresh-pressed suit, and damn if it doesn\u2019t look good on us. The Extreme Path is a dangerous one, and our only companions on it are death and despair. Whether we want to be or not, we\u2019re heralds of destruction with the guts to send the message. No reason to be afraid if you shut up and fall in line.<br /> <br /> <b>Why we should fear ourselves</b><br /> <br /> We\u2019re the eye of a storm, but look what happens to the rest of the world when we get together. It\u2019s hard to build a house when you\u2019re constantly repairing the foundation, so we keep building empires out of powder. We follow the path to keep ourselves in check, but maybe it\u2019s just a way to put a leash around our necks. What would happen if we left the path and made our own way? Would we be stronger \u2014 or would we implode?<br /> <br /> <b>Parent Clan: </b>Ventrue<br /> <br /> <b>Nicknames: </b>Wild Ones, Gangsters<br /> <br /> <b>Bloodline Bane (The Turbulent Curse): </b>A Warumono\u2019s adherence to extreme action makes it difficult to fly under the radar. Whenever a Wild One engages in violent action or draws blood, including feeding, she exudes an aura that makes her presence almost impossible to forget. For the rest of the scene, the Lost Visage fails. Mortal eyes are drawn to her, and any mundane rolls to act subtly, stealthily, or to escape notice are capped by her Humanity.<br /> <br /> <b>Disciplines:</b> Celerity, <i>Dominate</i>, Resilience, Vigor","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-01-2021, 04:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T16:38:30-07:00","id":1450748,"title":"","content":"Alright, slightly late start today, so let's get straight in to the Malocusians and Rotgrafen.<br /> <br /> Malocusians: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1444246-malocusians-2e-false-gods-ventrue-preview\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...entrue-preview</a><br /> <br /> Rotgrafen: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1444731-rotgrafen-2e-false-gods-ventrue-preview\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...entrue-preview</a><br /> <br /> (there were some dev changes since those excerpts went up, but mostly just a bit of editing, nothing big)<br /> <br /> So, the Malcusians. The <i>goddamn </i>Malocusians. This bloodline has been on my plate since <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/301087/Bloodlines-The-Devoted\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Bloodlines: The Devoted</b></a>, and they've utterly confounded me every time I revisit them. They were originally to be one of the big updates in <b>Devoted</b>, but I could never find good a hook for them. Part of the problem is that they're one of two territory-oriented bloodlines in <b>Invictus</b>: the Annunaku (<a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/281799/Bloodlines-The-Resurrected\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Bloodlines: The Resurrected</b></a>) share a <i>lot </i>of themes with the Malcusians, and, frankly, the Landlords do them better. The Annunaku have a fun backstory and a pretty cool Discipline (even for it's time), whereas the Malocusians have <i>no </i>backstory at all, and a really disjointed Discipline. They even have a similar bane to the Annunaku. The other part of the problem is that, for some reason, the Spiders' writeup only covers about a page and a half. Their Domus Discipline is far longer in terms of word and page count. I almost feel as though something was lost between final drafts and publication, or there was a need for big cuts (there are six bloodlines in <b>Invictus</b>!), but that's just speculation. There are some rumors in the book about a Ventrue branch of the Annunaku bloodline, which I...<i>think </i>is meant to be a hint about the Malocusians, but if so, it's rather opaque, and never really comes into play.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, the Malocusians ended up being one of the short updates in the Appendix of <b>BTD</b>, but I wasn't all that satisfied with that. Their power covered a lot of niches (Auspex, Nightmare, Protean, even Animalism), and I had trouble figuring out what to focus on even in a short update.<br /> <br /> Long story short, when we finally got to our Ventrue clan book, I wanted to take another crack at them. However, because I already had way too much on my plate, and because delegating your problems is one of the perks of being the boss, I assigned them to one of the other authors. However, he ran up against the same problems I did; i.e., there just wasn't much there. We had a long conversation about what to do with them, and we almost ended up turning them into a Horror, but that would've meant adding a new bloodline, and ultimately, I didn't want to have more new ones than old. I struggled a bit to find old bloodlines to update for this book, since most of the Ventrue with unique Disciplines had already been done in <b>Devoted</b>, and I wanted a good reason to do an update rather than just grabbing one for filler.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I'm glad we kept them as a bloodline, because I think they might be the best update in the book. Simplifying their Discipline spread to just adding Obfuscate covers a lot of the &quot;haunted house&quot; aesthetic they have going on, and it gives them access to both of <b>Vampire</b>'s area-of-effect powers (Lord of the Land and Oubliette). We also took their original bane and tweaked it a bit, making it not as big a deal for neonates, but a real issue for elders. One of our biggest concerns was making them more playable. Vampires who are bound to one place are very thematic, but they're hard for people to use as practical player characters.<br /> <br /> Here's the new Malocusian bane, as a little bonus excerpt:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>False Gods: Ventrue</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Bloodline Bane (The Sedentary Curse): </b>A Hermit\u2019s home is the most important place in the world, and to leave its bounds is akin to discarding a limb. If a Malocusian does not spend a point of Willpower upon exiting his domicile, he can only remain outside for (Humanity \u2013 Blood Potency) hours before the Beast starts clawing at his mind to return. After this time limit expires, and for every additional hour the Malocusian remains separated from his haven, he suffers a cumulative \u20131 to all rolls to resist frenzy, to a limit of \u20135 (though this still compounds with Conditions like Tempted). This penalty remains until the Spider has rested a full day within his home. If Blood Potency exceeds Humanity, Malocusians have a maximum of one hour before penalties accumulate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Anyway, that's the Malocusians. As for the Rotgrafen, I don't have much to say! Most of their update was quality of life stuff. Generally, that was making their backstory a little less definite and giving them a stronger mechanical edge. The biggest alteration is their bane. I never really cared for their aversion to fire; it was a little too Mekhet in 1E, and and ultimately it didn't work in Second Edition, because any Ordo Rotgrafen can just go buy Coil of the Ascendant and never have it matter again. Coils often present issues like that, and I didn't feel like writing around that one. Instead, I gave them something more active, a bane that maintained a frenzy-aspect while making it more roleplay-oriented.<br /> <br /> Their shapeshifting power was also a factor I thought the original lacked; if you're going to worship Loki and have Protean as a favoured Discipline, you should be able to shapeshift. That's the rule!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>False Gods: Ventrue</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Form of the Trickster<br /> (Protean \u2022\u2022\u2022)</b><br /> <br /> If Sigr\u00fan is to be believed, Loki granted her a fraction of his mutable nature when he saved her miserable life. With his Devotion, Rotgrafen can call upon his infamous ability to shapeshift, whether to infiltrate an enemy\u2019s stronghold or just discard the tyranny of fixed forms. Unlike many bloodline Devotions, this one isn\u2019t unique to the Rotgrafen, though their blood takes to it well. A multi-formed god wouldn\u2019t want just one group to enjoy his gifts, would he?<br /> <br /> This Devotion costs 2 Experiences to learn, or 1 if the character is a Rotgrafen.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Cost:</b> </span>2 Vitae<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dice Pool: </span></b>None<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\">Action: </span></b>Instant<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Duration:</b> </span>Indefinite<br /> <br /> Once a night, the vampire can transform into another person. They can be real or imaginary and of any gender, but they can\u2019t have any qualities beyond the human limit. To create a flawless imitation of a real individual, the Rotgrafen must consume their blood within the same night. Otherwise, she takes circumstantial penalties on any Social rolls to fool those familiar with the original, as defined by the Storyteller. This Devotion can bestow Giant and Small-Framed (<span style=\"color:#000000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span>, pp. 47 and 49), and either level of Striking Looks, but Sanctity of Merits does not apply. However, it can also temporarily remove these Merits, in which case it does apply if the vampire permanently redefines her form. Transformation lasts until the vampire chooses to revert to her default, but she can only take on one new face per night. She can also spend a Willpower while resting in her Unmarked Grave to redefine her default body.<br /> <br /> Note that the vampire\u2019s Physical Attributes and Skills remain the same, so she should be careful to play into her altered physique if she\u2019s undercover.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's it for today! Tomorrow, we'll talk about the Architects of the Monolith!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-03-2021, 06:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T07:48:21-07:00","id":1450815,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450748#post1450748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alright, slightly late start today, so let's get straight in to the Malocusians.<br /> <br /> Malocusians: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1444246-malocusians-2e-false-gods-ventrue-preview\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...entrue-preview</a><br /> <br /> So, the Malcusians. The <i>goddamn </i>Malocusians. This bloodline has been on my plate since <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/301087/Bloodlines-The-Devoted\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Bloodlines: The Devoted</b></a>, and they've utterly confounded me every time I revisit them. They were originally to be one of the big updates in <b>Devoted</b>, but I could never find good a hook for them. Part of the problem is that they're one of two territory-oriented bloodlines in <b>Invictus</b>: the Annunaku (<a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/281799/Bloodlines-The-Resurrected\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Bloodlines: The Resurrected</b></a>) share a <i>lot </i>of themes with the Malcusians, and, frankly, the Landlords do them better. The Annunaku have a fun backstory and a pretty cool Discipline (even for it's time), whereas the Malocusians have <i>no </i>backstory at all, and a really disjointed Discipline. They even have a similar bane to the Annunaku. The other part of the problem is that, for some reason, the Spiders' writeup only covers about a page and a half. Their Domus Discipline is far longer in terms of word and page count. I almost feel as though something was lost between final drafts and publication, or there was a need for big cuts (there are six bloodlines in <b>Invictus</b>!), but that's just speculation. There are some rumors in the book about a Ventrue branch of the Annunaku bloodline, which I...<i>think </i>is meant to be a hint about the Malocusians, but if so, it's rather opaque, and never really comes into play.</div> </div> </div> </div>Must admit i never really explored them, in no small part because i love to bits the Annunaki and used them more and ways than i care to count. If anything the idea of the rumors about a Ventrue branch of the Annunaku being obtuse hints about the Malocusians.... that made me notice them more than i ever did, i guess.<br /> <br /> Now have to check the 1e version just to see what i might make out of it or not. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T12:38:03-07:00","id":1450866,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1450815#post1450815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Must admit i never really explored them, in no small part because i love to bits the Annunaki and used them more and ways than i care to count. If anything the idea of the rumors about a Ventrue branch of the Annunaku being obtuse hints about the Malocusians.... that made me notice them more than i ever did, i guess.<br /> <br /> Now have to check the 1e version just to see what i might make out of it or not. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>The Annunaku have always sparked more interest in me too. I like that they're Gangrel who aren't wild and bestial, but who still fit the clan's themes really well. As an aside, since they gain Auspex in our updated version, and the Malocusians gain Obfuscate, there's a neat bit of counter-synergy there.<br /> <br /> Figuring out that (maybe) there's supposed to be a link between the two bloodlines helped me put the Spiders into perspective, but it really only raises more questions about why their write-up is so brief. There truly isn't much there in terms of backstory, which is surprising, because I'd say a characteristic of many 1E bloodlines is an overemphasis on histories.<br /> <br /> Anyway, let's leave the Malocusians in their rotting manses. Let's mistake the map for the territory, and talk Architects of the Monolith.<br /> <br /> Original preview: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1442812-the-architects-of-the-monolith-2e-false-gods-ventrue-preview\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...entrue-preview</a><br /> <br /> (As above, the text is a little different in the final book, but nothing huge.)<br /> <br /> So, anyone familiar with First Edition will know that the Masons were a bloodline. Our version broadens them into a covenant, albeit one that's comprised of a Ventrue majority (or at least a plurality), and which is led by a powerful Lord elder. On the surface, this change felt like an obvious choice, and I've received a lot of positive feedback about it. However, it wasn't so obvious to me at first! Here's how I came to it:<br /> <br /> It all started with a Discipline spread. I'm a bit fussy about bloodline Discipline combinations, and I try to adhere to certain unwritten rules that the game follows. For one, I don't like bloodlines to have more than two unique powers. You can't be good at everything, right? Plus, I think there's a point where if you pile on all the specialized powers, it muddies the thematic waters too much. You also lose some of the fun things available in the common Disciplines.<br /> <br /> For two, I don't like to give bloodlines inherent access to blood sorcery. I've broken this rule myself (i.e., the Csalad in <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/323224/Strange-Shades-Mekhet\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Strange Shades</b></a>), but even then I felt a bit conflicted about the choice. In my (admittedly pedantic) view, blood sorcery isn't something that should be inherent to a vampire. It's an act of deliberate, dark creation and alien intellect, not a side effect of being a dead thing that hunts the living. Maybe Cr\u00faac can be a bloodline Discipline sometimes (occasionally, rarely), but definitely not Theban Sorcery.<br /> <br /> Which brings me to the Architects of the Monolith. Not only did they have three unique Disciplines (Auspex, Dominate, and Gilded Cage), but one of them was a Theban Sorcery offshoot. So I started wracking my brain about how to solve this problem of my own creation. Do I just let that spread stand? It's an unwritten rule, not a hard one. Auspex could go, but it fits their archetype. Could I drop Gilded Cage and just make it something they have access to? But does that then mean other Kindred can access it? Do I just drop Gilded Cage entirely and give them some special Miracles?<br /> <br /> Decisions, decisions.<br /> <br /> This led to other issues, like their bane. If you've read other NMD books, you know we aren't precious about preserving old bloodline weaknesses, though we do try to at least thematically touch on the 1E versions. But the Architects just weren't clicking for me. Not only did they have a Derangement, they had a very setting-specific one, in that they're obsessed with their founder's plans. I kept having trouble making that interesting mechanically and narratively, this obsession with a character you'll likely never meet. I've actually translated the Masons' bane before as a snapshot update in <b>Bloodlines: The Devoted</b>, but I wasn't super happy with what I came up with. Astute readers may note that me being unsatisfied with the short translations is a running theme.<br /> <br /> Here's where I started to take a closer look at the old write-up, which formed a new question: <i>why </i>are they a bloodline? Why isn't this a minor covenant with a specialized blood sorcery? They have customs, organization, and an ideology. Other lineages have these things too, but the Architects, as written, felt much more like a political faction than a family. Now, bloodlines are probably the loosest of the so-called Z-splats, in that they can be just about anything politically and socially. I tend to find that's a strength of their design (YMMV), and one that's a lot easier to write for than, for example, Lodges and Legacies. But, I felt like the bloodline format wasn't doing the Architects any favors. It was constraining them in a way that it doesn't for other more politically organized bloodlines. Plus, I didn't want to just retread the Csalad, which was probably my biggest concern beyond the writer's block.<br /> <br /> Turning them into a covenant would make their Derangement into a choice, not an involuntary mental condition. That got me thinking about some real life ideologies; a few of you will probably notice that my write-up is kind of a parody/horror version of (among other things) Objectivism, and the Carpenter of Ayn Rand. Rather than have the Masons be &quot;crazy&quot;, they just believe in their cause to the Nth degree. The kind of vampire who's attracted to their movement is going to fall for it <i>hard</i>.<br /> <br /> Anyway, that's a long-winded way of saying their Discipline spread annoyed me so I made them a covenant.<br /> <br /> If you prefer the Masons as a bloodline, and if I might insert a non-self-serving plug for once, the folks over at Darker Days Radio did an update of the Masons in <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/298932/The-Secret-Frequency-Files\" target=\"_blank\"><b>The Secret Frequency Files</b></a>. I ultimately prefer the Architects as a covenant, but I quite like the bloodline's presentation there, as it fits the original version's &quot;hidden masters of the world&quot; themes. That version is closer mechanically to the original, but their take on the backstory is quite different. There's also my short update in <b>Bloodlines: The Devoted</b>, so you've got lots of choices for bringing the Architects of the Monolith into Second Edition.<br /> <br /> Alright, that's it for today. Tomorrow, we'll be going into the other covenant, the insidious Legio Noctis...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-03-2021, 06:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T13:28:45-07:00","id":1450979,"title":"","content":"Happy Friday all! Time for our last excerpt of the week.<br /> <br /> The Legio Noctis are a brand new covenant of soldiers and secret agents, though they aren't entirely without precedent. They claim to have roots in the Camarilla's Legio Mortuum (<i>sic</i>) and the medieval Grim Battalion, though those connections are implied to be complicated at best and tenuous at worst.<br /> <br /> Like the Typhos Institute, the Legio went through a fair few changes over the course of development. Originally the concept was a lot more secretive and conspiratorial, and few vampires were meant to know they even existed. They were also implied to be EVERYWHERE, planting agents in countless domains and having a top down structure that worked with laser-like focus to take over the All Night Society.<br /> <br /> That wasn't a bad start, but I'm wary of including antagonists of that scale in <b>Requiem</b>; an all-powerful enemy is hard to make interesting. It also makes it difficult to justify their secret nature if they're actually out there conquering things. So, we toned them down a bit to be more ambiguous in their ultimate structure, and a little less unified than they tell their grunts. That, and I wanted to distinguish them more from the Architects of the Monolith, who also have a bit of that &quot;secretly taking over the world while you aren't looking&quot; theme. The author and I also felt the first version ultimately ended up being too fascistic, which was a theme neither of us were comfortable with exploring in any overt fashion. That's the danger of doing these covenant write-ups partly in the first person, as sometimes the dramatic irony/perspective isn't clear without going really over the top. And even though this covenant was meant mostly for Storytellers to use as an antagonist, I did want to leave the door open for player characters \u2014 <i>if </i>the troupe is down with it \u2014 so that meant softening them.<br /> <br /> In the end, the Legio are more like a vampire Blackwater (or the CIA, really). Their ultimate goal has more to do with Kindred globalism than militarism. They still want to reforge the Camarilla, but they do so through shadowy imperialism and false flags rather than having the goal ruling over domains. They place pawns where they need them and create ever larger convocations. How successful they've been is up to you, as is just how sincere their praetors really are about this whole New Camarilla thing.<br /> <br /> The one thing that didn't really change in drafts was CLOTs, apart from some mechanical tweaking. I really liked the idea of pseudo-Devotion tactics, and I think they're a really fun toolkit.<br /> <br /> Alright, that's it for now. The following is slightly abridged, as the Legio have a fairly long entry, so check out the book if you're interested in getting all details. Next week I think we'll start looking at the ghoul families, starting with the Crassus!<br /> <br /> <b>The Legio Noctis: The Last Legion</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;No terms. No quarter.</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>The big picture</b><br /> <br /> The Legio Noctis is an organization of Kindred mercenaries and black ops specialists. They would say they\u2019re merely Soldiers, but they work more like surgeons to remove threats both external and internal. It\u2019s their duty to protect, whether that means icing werewolves or committing regicide. The Army of Night deploys globally, funded by their work as operators and professional ratfuckers to the All Night Society. The CIA can only dream of their regime-change tally. If you know where to look, and you need to be rid of the upper echelons (or just the weird shit), the Legion is ready, armed with centuries of war profiteering. They\u2019ve invested in weapons technology, purchased politicians, brokered arms deals, and made a buck off conflicts ranging from the American Revolution to the Cold War. When in doubt, always bet on red.<br /> <br /> Officially, they\u2019re apolitical, and they\u2019ll lend their might to any Kindred who can pay their price. Their forces have backed militant collectivists, suppressed revolutions for absolute monarchs, spread vampiric perversions of neo-liberal democracy, and propped up pagan theocrats whose sole ethos is watching their enemies burn in pleasing ways. Ideology has little to do with their work. Whatever else a Soldier might believe, the Legion demands dedication to its own philosophy above all others: Victory and Death. The one should sacrifice for all; the war never ends.<br /> <br /> But that\u2019s the thing, isn\u2019t it? It\u2019s all well and good to be perpetually at war, but what\u2019s the victory condition? The Legio Noctis has an answer \u2014 change. And once that change comes, the Legion can usher in utopia. Every puppet prince they place, every coalition government they build, every \u201cnew\u201d political movement they usher into power is another tick on their timeline. The true goal is to become obsolete. The Last Legion.<br /> <br /> Under the lens of a bellicose yet big tent philosophy, the All Night Society is fractured, narrow-minded, and impotent, forced to hide from humanity because they\u2019re too selfish to work as one. The modern world is a miracle of connection and communication, yet most Kindred are afraid to ever leave their tiny domains. They\u2019re given eternity and spend it trapped in city-shaped tombs. The Legio Noctis wants to fix that. Behind its secretive veil of blood money, it works to build a New Camarilla, a global Kindred empire forged by the might of the first\u2019s self-appointed heirs.<br /> <br /> <b>Where we came from</b><br /> <br /> We call him the Nameless Soldier. Oh, we have dozens of epithets and titles for him, but none have ever been confirmed. The most popular story says he was a Ventrue \u2014 perhaps the first \u2014 calling himself Lucius Romanicus Nox, a patrician who fought his way through the Julii\u2019s doom and came out the other side different. But the truth isn\u2019t important. He is the king who walks among the rank and file. He is the general on the front line. And he saved us from ourselves in our darkest hour.<br /> <br /> For more than a thousand years, the Legion of the Dead was a shield against barbarism. For the Kindred of Rome, it enforced the law and stood tall against forces no one else was brave enough \u2014 or capable enough \u2014 to fight. Indeed, the humblest of the Camarilla\u2019s Wings limped on after the rise of Christianity and the fall of Rome, but it wasn\u2019t enough. After Rome fell, some of the Legion regrouped in Constantinople and restructured its ranks. It kept the peace between hundreds of Kindred refugees and the Lancea et Sanctum, who hoarded wealth and blood from the starving Roman masses. It was a cruel, grueling duty, to ignore the pleas of friends and kin, but a duty nonetheless.<br /> <br /> Yet we were betrayed. Not by barbarians or spies, but by the Sanctified and our own leaders. They shattered our unity to create a \u201cLegion of the Damned\u201d made up of our most formidable and devout, then left the rest of us with little but a burnt offering. Divided, we could not save the Camarilla as it died a Final Death, gasping in the gutters through a slit throat.<br /> <br /> We would have fallen too if the Nameless Soldier had not arrived.<br /> <br /> As riots grew and oceans of Vitae flooded the streets, he appeared and took command of the shattered ranks. For the first time, the Legion defended not an empire, but itself, and because of that, we triumphed. This was the first lesson we learned from the Soldier, and we shall never forget.<br /> <br /> From the ashes, a new Legion was born. They called us the Grim Battalion \u2014 assassins, killers, mercenaries, and worse. Perhaps we were, but we were free. Under the Soldier\u2019s Laws, we reorganized and revitalized. We built our strength until our power grew enough that we posed a threat to the new covenants. They sought to enslave and destroy us, but that was the second lesson. The foolish fear the righteous; the liar hates the truth. So, we vanished into history. We were not gone, though. We merely adapted.<br /> <br /> <b>Our practices</b><br /> <br /> Outwardly, we\u2019re exactly what we seem to be, an elite band of professional mercenaries and secret agents that takes care of problems other vampires can\u2019t deal with on their own. Is your prince\u2019s mind infirm with the weight of ages, yet still all-too-powerful? Have the Acolytes gone too far with their unholy perversions? Are the Dragons doing anything? If you have the coin, you have our swords. Or rather, our Counter-Kindred Logistical Operation Tactics (CLOTs). These strategies endow our squads with the ability to overpower even the most terrifying powers of the Damned with our deadliest asset: Teamwork.<br /> <br /> Inwardly, we have bigger fish to fry. The New Camarilla isn\u2019t going to build itself, and the Kindred require a strong hand to guide its construction. Once the fighting\u2019s done, the operation\u2019s only just beginning. Sometimes we leave behind a force for \u201cpeacekeeping,\u201d or to make sure elections run the right way. More often, we\u2019ve been in a domain for decades, engineering whatever crisis required our services in the first place. We love a false flag.<br /> <br /> Apart from our more overt work placing loyalists to fill in the political vacuums we create, our field agents position themselves in their domains so they can become not just useful, but linchpins to their courts. To a member, infiltrators are trained to hide in plain sight: The sheriff\u2019s deputy who keeps a ledger of the edicts his boss ignores. The wide-eyed convert who asks earnest questions of the bishop to sow unease among the flock. The beautiful courtier who guides guileless hunters to her hit list. These are Soldiers, one and all, and they\u2019re ready on the frontlines before our ground troops ever touch pavement.<br /> <br /> <i>Vae victis</i>, as the Romans say.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-07T13:45:17-07:00","id":1451251,"title":"","content":"Welcome back, everyone!<br /> <br /> So, today we're starting our look into ghoul families with the Crassus. These excerpts will be a tad shorter than the others, as the ghouls don't cover nearly as much page count, but I'll try to include the really fun bits.<br /> <br /> The Crassus first appeared in <b>Ghouls</b>, way back in 2005, and are one of two Ventrue families; the Alley Men were Ventrue instead of Gangrel for reasons that are a bit beyond me, but they got updated in <b>Half-Damned</b>, so that's neither here nor there. The original version of the Crassus was... hm. You know what, I'm not gonna dress this up: they were boring. Imagine a Ventrue ghoul family, make them a bit gaudy, add in some edgy crap, and you'll have the Crassus in their original form. I really try not to be snitty about the older books, because it's way too easy to dunk on these things with 20/20 hindsight, but boy oh boy has <b>Ghouls </b>burned me every time I give it the benefit of the doubt. That's a discussion for another day, though.<br /> <br /> That said, the Crassus weren't all that objectionable. Mostly (we'll get to that). They were just a bit dull. They also presented a problem of having a really intrinsic Ancient Rome connection. Because <b>Ghouls </b>came out two years before <b>Requiem for Rome</b>, the Ventrue are assumed to have been running the show in the Camarilla. Rather than write around that problem, I decided to embrace it. The Crassus say the vampire who made them ghouls was a Ventrue, not a Julii, and their curse kinda backs them up. Part of the hook of the bloodline is how that all jives with the canon of the game; you'll even find a story seed about it.<br /> <br /> The other issue was what to do with their niche. When I say the original entry was dull, I mostly mean that they're generic. They're Ventrue ghouls who are wealthy and trashy. The original entry has some gross stuff about them being toys for vampires to play with, which, in addition to being very icky, isn't an interesting character to play. To put it another way, they were totally reactive. So, they needed a rebranding.<br /> <br /> I don't quite remember where I came up with luck manipulation, but I suspect it had to do with their logo. Their symbol resembles a wheel of fortune, and I think it must've got me wondering about how these trashy ghouls actually acquired their wealth back in Rome. I also had just finished some Malkavia drafts, and that might've influenced what's going on with them too.<br /> <br /> (Someone mentioned above that the entry doesn't bring up the Atariya, which seems like an obvious connection. In short, I didn't remember they existed! That said, after looking at <b>Hurt Locker</b>, I'm okay with not including that link; the Crassus are luck <i>thieves</i>, whereas the Atariya are &quot;just&quot; lucky. Plus I don't think every similar phenomena in the CofD has to know about each other.)<br /> <br /> The only other change I made was softening their connection to the Invictus. They probably are mostly Invictus ghouls considering what they do, but I wanted them to be a bit more open to the covenants. You can still treat them as Invictus-only without any trouble, though.<br /> <br /> At any rate, once I got past the old version, the Crassus were a lot of fun to write, so I hope you enjoy the excerpt! Tomorrow, I'll be introducing you to the DeRoses, who'd just <i>love </i>to talk to you. About anything, really...<br /> <br /> <b>Crassus: The ones who play the odds</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Call it.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <b>Where we came from</b><br /> <br /> Once upon a time in Ancient Rome, Quintus Crassus was an architect, a drunk, a gambler, and shit at all three. The last one in particular. Apparently he\u2019d made it his life mission to squander a vast inheritance; when Quintus wasn\u2019t reneging on a contract, he was dicking around the circuses, or anywhere else that would let him throw sesterces at a bad bet. He did have one talent, though, if you could call it that. As much as the dice mocked him, they reserved a greater spite for those who took him to the cleaners. Maybe the Fates saw poor sportsmanship in fleecing such a loser, but those who beat Quintus too badly suffered unexpected turns. One might break a finger on the marble table as she pulled in her winnings, or soil fine new clothes in horseshit as they crossed the road from the races. On one occasion, a centurion dropped a dagger through his crotch.<br /> <br /> Quintus had no control over these retributions, but he enjoyed the petty thrill of them, at least until they got him banned from every gambling house in the Eternal City. However, it also caught the attention of a vampire called Lysander. The elder had laired in Rome for almost as long as the Camarilla, but was not one of the Propinqui. The Crassus family chronicle speaks of some ill will he held for the Julii, though it\u2019s vague on detail. Quintus\u2019s bad luck intrigued Lysander, and he invited the fool to come gamble with him at the Circus Maximus. When a brazier fell and almost burned a hole through Lysander\u2019s chest, right after he\u2019d finished winning the mortal\u2019s last copper, he knew he\u2019d found an asset.<br /> <br /> As a ghoul, Quintus\u2019s star rose. The Kindred blood in his veins put him on the straight and narrow, and he began to win his gambles. Or rather, others started losing them. Quintus never did improve his talent for playing the odds, but his opponents got a lot worse. Maybe the Vitae focused his fortune, or maybe the high just cleared his booze-addled brain enough to weaponize it \u2014 but Crassus never thought much about the cause. As long as he got his fix, anyone he touched fell to some random blight.<br /> <br /> The modern Crassus downplay this history, exaggerating their founder\u2019s rise to wealth with tales of real canny estate deals and glad-handing with Julius Caesar. Nonetheless, Quintus\u2019s many children bore the same luck-sapping touch, and it\u2019s still their only real talent. As for Lysander? Never heard from again after the reign of Vespasian, but he got what he wanted. The night the Camarilla fell, Crassus courtesans passed among the Senex, offering their wrists to all who desired them.<br /> <br /> <b>Who we are tonight</b><br /> <br /> Post-Rome, the Crassus rebranded and expanded. No longer just gamblers and luck vampires for hire, they built an empire on always being able to eke out a win or break even. The All Night Society employs Crassus ghouls as both hexes to throw at enemies and piggy banks for when the Danse Macabre proves a poor investment. With interest, of course.<br /> <br /> The Age of Discovery only added to the family\u2019s coffers. The Crassus were early investors in the New World\u2019s plunder, and one of their branches was fortunate enough to be on the right side of the Revolutionary War. These saloon owners watered and grifted Minutemen, set up gambling dens in the Dakotas, and made a small foothold in the early stock exchange with uncanny investments. Most modern Crassus descend from or were members of this group. Unbound from European class concerns, they embraced a corrosive American Dream, amassing vast riches without regard to taste or scruples.<br /> <br /> Kindred and mortal alike know not to cross the self-described Caesars, even if they do make tempting targets. The Crassus are forever nouveau riche, as if the reality of having a millennia-old fortune never quite sank in. Indeed, their modern nickname comes more from their Vegas interests than their imperial past. The Coolers are sleazy at best and social blights at worst, tacking their name to all manner of tacky projects, from Roman-themed resorts to American-style plutocracy. And yet, the cash keeps flowing upward. Consequences are poor people problems, and on the rare occasion fate comes knocking, weaseling out is just a matter of tapping the right shoulder.<br /> <br /> The Crassus do fear one thing, however: the Ventrue. The Caesars whore out to any vampire willing to give them a taste, but they\u2019re bound to the Lords in a way few other Ventrue ghoul families are. The clan holds all the trumps, and (not for lack of trying) the Crassus have never found a way to cash out. The Lords only lend their blood, and if the Crassus displease them, they\u2019re prepared to take it back.<br /> <br /> With interest, of course.<br /> <br /> <b>Benefit: </b>The Crassus are richer than Croesus, but not out of any business sense. They strip the luck from their rivals\u2019 souls and know just enough to spend it in all the right places. At character creation, Coolers gain the Thief of Fate Merit (<b>Chronicles of Darkness</b>, p. 60). Ventrue Kindred are immune to this effect, as is any vampire with whom a Crassus ghoul shares a third-stage blood bond. Other family members are also immune. In addition, Caesars may purchase Dynasty Membership within their local branch, substituting their family Status for Clan Status.<br /> <br /> PS: Also, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25955-somethingfishy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar25955_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25955\" data-vbnamecard=\"25955\">SomethingFishy</a>'s illustration for the Crassus character might be my favourite one in the book. He embodies the word douchebag.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-09-2021, 08:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-08T17:22:18-07:00","id":1451406,"title":"","content":"Hello! Late excerpt today, because I forgot I had a previous engagement smack dab in the middle of the day, so this will be fairly efficient!<br /> <br /> The DeRoses are one of two completely new ghoul families in the book, spies and informants who use their natural (or unnatural) good looks to manipulate their marks. What's fun about this family is that they press a little bit into what you might think of as a Daeva thematic space, but they do it with a Ventrue twist. The DeRoses aren't really interested in sexuality and performance; they only care about collecting more power. They focus on an erotic element, but it's almost entirely a front \u2014 or so they tell themselves. I also like that they muddy the water on what constitutes a biological ghoul family vs. a social one. I'm always a fan of breaking down and blurring these neat little categories the game uses as shorthands (clan, bloodline, ghoul family). Not everything fits together so cleanly in the All Night Society.<br /> <br /> The DeRoses changed very little in development, so apart from some more detail on their relationship with the vampires and Ventrue, they're pretty close to their first draft. The only big change was their weakness. Thematically it didn't change very much, but mechanically, they actually have what was originally the Crassus drawback, before I decided to play more into their fate thievery aspect.<br /> <br /> So, enjoy the excerpt. Tomorrow we'll be looking at the Omokage of Japan, and then we'll be moving on to the two Horrors. Also, if you haven't done so, please consider dropping us a review or a star rating if you've enjoyed the book! As I've said before, it's super helpful to getting eyes on our work. Also, make sure you have the most recent pdf. I've made a few corrections since the release. There's probably going to be one more in the near future, but I'll make sure to announce that here (these excerpts also help me notice typos). Anyway, here's the DeRoses:<br /> <br /> <b>DeRose: The ones who say they love you</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;C\u2019mon, you know you can tell me anything.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <b>Where we came from</b><br /> <br /> The DeRose family originated somewhere around the Franco-Italian border in the late 19th century, though they\u2019re not exactly a family in the traditional sense. DeRose was once a title granted to favored servants of an elite Ventrue brothel, many of whose owners had worked as courtesans and procurers in life. These Lords weren\u2019t interested in the hunt, and their living talents in the sex trade taught them it\u2019s a simple matter to get prey to come to you. Some Kindred keep a regular supply of consenting, eager blood bags \u2014 and if those bags can handle business during daylight hours? All the better.<br /> <br /> A common conceit among Flowers is that their origin story differs depending on how well the teller can hold their liquor. What\u2019s known for certain is that a minor Lord among this group, one Guillaume Morceaux, took a liking to one of these DeRose servants, Madeline. She had a special knack for gaining people\u2019s trust, and Guillaume recognized how useful that could be if he put her in his personal employ. At least, that\u2019s how it started. If you were to ask a sober DeRose, she\u2019d recount a tale of true love overcoming the tribulations of circumstance and global conflict. However, get one talking after a few dozen Dark \u2019n\u2019 Stormies and you might instead hear about an overconfident regnant who didn\u2019t know when he was being played, and the clever servant with a skill for feigning affection. Both versions have the couple fleeing to America to escape the Great War, and both end in Guillaume\u2019s ever so tragic disappearance, not long after he and Madeline started to expand their little family.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the truth, a family name was born. Madeline DeRose became the House\u2019s first matriarch, and her \u201cchildren\u201d inherited her unique talent for getting others to open up. Over time, the family spread to most major metropolitan centers to ply their particular craft. For those who know where to look among the darker corners of a city, the House of DeRose is always ready with acceptance and love. For a price.<br /> <br /> <b>Who we are tonight</b><br /> <br /> If you know what to look for, it\u2019s easy to recognize a Flower. Are they the sexiest person you\u2019ve ever met? The one you and you alone have dreamed of all your miserable life? Does that feeling immediately fade away once you take your eyes off them? If the answer to these questions is yes, you\u2019ve got yourself a DeRose.<br /> <br /> On the surface, the family\u2019s business is sex work: high-end brothels, exclusive strip clubs, burlesque revues, and other hosting services. However, their primary purpose is intelligence. Each DeRose matriarch (a position that falls somewhere between den mother, casting director, madame, and spymaster) controls a nest of agents trained in the arts of seduction and snitching. For a hundred years, the DeRoses have made postcoital conversation into a whisper network empire.<br /> <br /> They don\u2019t sell everything they hear, or even most of it \u2014 they never would\u2019ve got where they are tonight if they were indiscriminate \u2014 but the dirt they do trade is always a cut above. The Flowers have brought down everything from small town mayors to state governors, and even a few princes. If you can pay, they don\u2019t care who takes the fall.<br /> <br /> Most DeRose chapterhouses are a collection of runaways, vagabonds, deviants, and biological offspring\u2026 which isn\u2019t to say they adopt just any stray who ends up on their doorstep. Prospective DeRoses go through a trial period where they take on the least glamorous jobs associated with running the hottest gentle*man\u2019s club in town, usually in return for room and board. Once they prove themselves, they\u2019re given a special audience with the chapter\u2019s matriarch, wherein they\u2019re officially inducted into the family with the blood of the chapter\u2019s regnant. DeRose matriarchs look for a broad range of traits in their initiates. Determination, wit, and natural gravitas are sure to catch her attention, but the most important thing is ambition. Initiates need to show how badly they want in, and they need to do it by making themselves useful \u2014 and beautiful. The recruits who wash out first are always the ones who get complacent.<br /> <br /> Family members are quick to promise wavering initiates that it\u2019s only a matter of time before their times come. In reality, it\u2019s not uncommon for them to be led on for years, and if an initiate ever becomes more trouble than they\u2019re worth, one day, they\u2019ll just be gone. Whenever this happens, the family reassures anyone who asks that their erstwhile companion simply chose to leave of their own accord. Strangely enough, these disappearances are almost always followed by a visit from some influential Ventrue client. But that\u2019s probably unrelated.<br /> <br /> Most chapterhouses are extensions of a local Lord\u2019s holdings. They either serve as spies for her political interests or easy money to fill her coffers. Sex, secrets \u2014 whatever. It\u2019s all the same when the credit card gets swiped. Some matriarchs make arrangements with members of other clans, and indeed, Daeva often covet these ghouls, though Flowers tend to see Serpents as the kind of fawning twits they pretend to be on the job (or maybe they don\u2019t like what they see in the mirror). In the end, the House gets on best with the Lords. Romantic Flowers say it\u2019s the memory of their dear departed Guillaume calling out to those of his blood, though the more cynical just note that cold bastards tend to work well with other cold bastards. All business; no sentiment. Game recognize game, as they say.<br /> <br /> The DeRoses value their family above all else, but who they call family and who actually is family are two different things. A certain amount of deception is integral to the fantasy they sell. That\u2019s why they\u2019ve flourished. They know exactly what you want to hear. When they say it, you believe it \u2014 and if you don\u2019t? There are always workarounds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-08-2021, 08:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T14:08:02-07:00","id":1451488,"title":"","content":"Alright, let's get right into the Omokage.<br /> <br /> This was another one that changed a lot in second drafts. The original iteration was basically what the final version <i>claim </i>they are: a celebrated order of servants who carry on a tradition that goes back for centuries. For various reasons, this wasn't really working. It was too obvious a take on a Ventrue ghoul family, and it also had way too much overlap with the Kobayashi from <b>Better Feared</b>.<br /> <br /> In redlines, I asked the author if he could turn this idea on its head: What if these elite retainers are hiding something? Or, what if they're the ones who are actually in charge? From that, he came up with the idea of turning their history into a fraud, something these ghouls use to trick naive neonates. Similar to the Warumono, the Omokage are criminals; unlike the Warumono, they keep that under a tight lid, and have no illusions about honour among thieves.<br /> <br /> One element that did survive redlines was the concept of Houses. It was retuned a bit for the final, but I really liked the idea of ghoul families with differing advantages based on certain social divisions, but a unifying drawback and Touchstone. That's something I might want to include in more families as we move on with this series.<br /> <br /> So, here's the Omokage. Tomorrow, would you all like to hear about the Faithful, the intelligent hellhounds, or the Mnemovores, originally from <b>Night Horrors: The Wicked Dead</b>?<br /> <br /> <b>Omokage: The ones who take care of everything</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Relax. I'll handle it.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <b>Where we came from</b><br /> <br /> The Omokage would like the rest of Tokyo to believe they\u2019re the remnants of an ancient family of vassals, dating back to the time of Japan\u2019s feuding nobility. Their humbleness and apparent refinement would seem to support this claim, as would the traditional trappings of their three Houses. In reality, however, the Omokage have existed for less than two decades, and were created almost by mistake, thanks to one young Ventrue\u2019s reach exceeding his grasp.<br /> <br /> Hiro Yamaguchi thought he\u2019d found a way to claim some power in Tokyo\u2019s underworld, outside the grasp of the monolithic zaibatsu controlling most of the city. He attempted to bond a minor branch of a local yakuza group, Matsuba-kai, hoping to install himself as the gang\u2019s leader and have them spread his influence.<br /> <br /> He could hardly have picked worse victims if he\u2019d tried. The gangsters were already well-versed in confidence games and using addiction as a form of control; they didn\u2019t need to know about Disciplines and Vincula to notice the same tactics. When confronted, Yamaguchi panicked and attacked, killing a pair of capos. In return, he was beaten and stabbed nearly to Final Death, then starved and interrogated by the furious Matsuba-kai. Eventually, the yakuza learned all about the Kindred and their unique relationship with the human businesses in Tokyo. The gang seized Yamaguchi\u2019s assets and discovered the aspiring Lord was an avid collector of historic Japanese artifacts and literature.<br /> <br /> From their new knowledge and stolen wealth, a plan gradually emerged among the remaining Matsuba-kai, many of whom Yamaguchi had turned into ghouls in the course of his bid for control. Now that they knew about this world, they wanted a piece of it \u2014 but they couldn\u2019t count on finding another useful idiot. However, they saw how static the Kindred power structure was, and how hungry younger vampires were for an edge, especially when dealing with the human-run Hototogisu. Remembering how Yamaguchi had underestimated them, the gang realized there was a way they could use Kindred money and power to their advantage. They just had to make certain the vampires thought they were still the ones in charge.<br /> <br /> <b>Who we are tonight</b><br /> <br /> The gang chose the name Omokage (oh-moh-KAH-gay), a word for vestiges and fragments, to evoke an air of tragic mystery. Presenting themselves as an venerable family of Stewards seeking worthy masters, the former gangsters use Yamaguchi\u2019s collection of artifacts, Internet research, and good old-fashioned hustling to build their reputation. With the Kindred\u2019s reverence for tradition turned against them, the family has found fertile ground among the youngest Ventrue of Tokyo, who wouldn\u2019t know an ancient lineage if it bit them.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s not <i>entirely </i>a scam. The Omokage might not have any real centuries-old traditions, but they know Tokyo and its underworld intimately, and can bridge the gap between Kindred operators and Hototogisu affiliates like few others. The first deals they broker for a new client are always in the vampire\u2019s favor, to build trust. The fact that these transactions also bring money into the family\u2019s own illicit dealings isn\u2019t a detail they \u201ctrouble\u201d their regnants with.<br /> <br /> In keeping with their pseudo-feudal trappings, the Omokage have divided their membership into Houses, each one specializing in a different service to the Kindred. Houses provide all manner of servants, from butlers to majordomos, but the following are their specialties.<br /> <br /> Crane House supplies bookies, money-launderers, and crooked lawyers acting as \u201cscholars\u201d who can expand a Lord\u2019s financial holdings. The wealthy rarely handle their own money; they have people for that, and Crane House is happy to be those people for a cut.<br /> <br /> Fox House makes informants out of escorts and hosts. The sex trade in Tokyo is vast, ubiquitous, and mostly ignored by mortal law-enforcement. One of the main trappings of a successful vampire is being thronged by attractive humans to feed from, and so Fox is the most consistently popular of the three Houses.<br /> <br /> Tiger House trains bodyguards, cleaners, and legbreakers, supposedly to safeguard their masters\u2019 Requiems. In reality, such muscle is almost never necessary, but the image of being flanked by goons in mirror-shades is one many young Lords crave. Arriving with protection implies you\u2019re someone worth protecting.<br /> <br /> The Omokage have invented histories, founders, and rivalries for their Houses, all the better to make Kindred think they\u2019re in on something privileged and secret. Marks are made to feel like the Houses are competing for their attention and favor, and that the highest prestige an Omokage can earn is being selected for service. In the beginning, it was just the best actors in the gang who would get themselves hired, the better to play the part of the humble, loyal servant. All the Houses would share resources to ensure the new ghoul would appear hyper-competent, as the division between the gang members was an illusion. However, in a case of life imitating art, in recent years the Houses have actually grown to more resemble the elite institutions they claim to be. With some Stewards having worked in their professions for years, and as various Disciplines becoming more prevalent among them, the three Houses are gaining some real bite to back up their bark. Their put-on rivalries, however, remain just that \u2014 at least for now.<br /> <br /> All this is to get their Kindred clients, usually neonate Ventrue, to trust the Omokage with their resources and decisions. As humans (mostly) there are places in Tokyo Stewards can access that vampires simply can\u2019t. On the mortal side of things, having Kindred powers provides the family an edge dealing with rival criminal concerns. Their status as servants also means Stewards aren\u2019t usually invited to the truly important Kindred-exclusive meetings, but that suits them fine. They want to avoid situations where they might have to interact with really old vampires, as such creatures could easily disprove their ancient lineage if they cared to look closely. However, if something does go wrong, the Omokage are more than willing to cut a deal to save themselves by selling out their clients.<br /> <br /> Ostensibly, the vampire is always in charge of the relationship, but as trust grows and a Steward ghoul gains more and more oversight over their master\u2019s operation, well\u2026 what\u2019s the difference between representing a vampire\u2019s interests and actually just being in charge? Exactly.<br /> <br /> <b>Stereotypes:</b><ul><li><b>Daeva:</b> Learn from them, but at a distance. They do our routine as naturally as they used to breathe.</li> <li><b>Gangrel:</b> Best avoided. The Savages think they don\u2019t need help, and get testy when you imply otherwise.</li> <li><b>Mekhet: </b>Never give them a reason to look.</li> <li><b>Nosferatu: </b>Flattery doesn\u2019t work. Sympathy, on the other hand\u2026</li> <li><b>Ventrue: </b>Sometimes it\u2019s hard to tell who\u2019s hooked who, but as long as the blood keeps flowing, everybody\u2019s happy.</li> </ul><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T14:25:36-07:00","id":1451575,"title":"","content":"Hey all! I'm a little behind schedule on other things today, so just quick excerpt today!<br /> <br /> The Faithful are sapient, ghoul-like dogs who sometimes arise due to a combination of Animalism and Ventrue blood. A bit of a Lord's ambition rubs off on their loyal hounds, birthing something with a terrifying, all-too-human intelligence.<br /> <br /> This Horror didn't change too much in development, other than some mechanical edits. We also added their Hellhounds nickname because the word &quot;Faithful&quot; was beginning to lose all meaning. As a sidenote, the author took some inspiration from the Amaranthine cats in <b>Night Horrors: Spilled Blood</b>, so if you're looking to have a cat and dog alliance against the Kindred, look no further.<br /> <br /> Here's the Faithful. Tomorrow I'll be back with the Mnemovores, and I think that might be the last excerpt!(?)<br /> <br /> <b>The Faithful: Biting the Hand That Feeds</b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Oh, a doggie. Good boy? OH GOD\u2014&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <b>AKA: </b>Hellhounds, <i>Canis lupus dominorum</i><br /> <br /> <b>Background</b><br /> <br /> The Faithful are products of centuries of selective breeding, Vitae, and exposure to Animalism. A portion of a Lord\u2019s blood and intellect infuses a Hellhound with a new spark of life, and it becomes self-aware at the level of a human being. The resulting creature is cunning, strong, and either very useful or very dangerous to the vampire who created it.<br /> <br /> This awakening can be a painful, isolating process, leaving many of these animals broken-hearted or alone among the feral minds of other beasts. Desperate for connection, they seek comfort with other intelligent creatures, often forging a single-minded, obsessive bond with their chosen masters. Their heightened mental capacity lets them surpass any expectation, read subtle intentions, and react in a precise, strategic manner to threats, making them ideal guardians and companions.<br /> <br /> Sometimes Lords are wise enough to see what they have in front of them, breeding entire bloodlines of Hellhounds. However, this can create unforeseen dangers, as hidden within each of these beasts is a hunger for the sticky-sweet Vitae within a vampire\u2019s veins. Even the most intelligent dog is still a beast, and sometimes, good dogs go bad.<br /> <br /> <b>Bloodhounds</b><br /> <br /> As Faithful age, time takes its toll on their bodies, and the only thing that slows this is the consumption of Vitae. It begins slowly \u2014 hunting animals, then mortals, then a less favored ghoul. Perhaps the Hellhound tries stalking one of its master\u2019s blood dolls to see it craves the taste or the hunt. Step by step, the dog tests the limits until it grows bold and hungry enough to hunt Kindred. Once that step is complete, the Faithful is empowered to continue its predation in secret, loyally serving its master all the while. But, if no one\u2019s left to feed from save their Lords, loyalty may not be enough.<br /> <br /> It almost sounds funny: A dog raised by monsters turns on its masters. The reality is less amusing. Hellhounds are thinking, intelligent beasts. They\u2019ve paid dutiful attention, and know the Kindred better than they know themselves. Some gain a hunger for a particular type of Vitae, or turn on abusive masters, murdering them and swearing bloody vengeance on the clan that birthed them. Others are left alone through the vicissitudes of fate, and others still remain at the sides of powerful elders capable of feeding their inhuman appetites.<br /> <br /> As such, patterns start to form. There are three distinct \u201cbreeds\u201d of Faithful:<br /> <br /> <b>The Unleashed </b>are rogue Hellhounds whose masters have cast them away. The tenacious old dog that survives euthanasia; the abandoned puppy who\u2019s grown too hungry for a young Lord to feed; the mother about to bring a litter of monsters into the world. The Unleashed survive and take revenge on their masters by targeting their mortal families, ghouls, or childer, before finally taking a bite out of their betrayers.<br /> <br /> <b>The Strays </b>were once proud servants whose loyalty survived the Final Death or torpor of their owners. The dutiful watcher at the master\u2019s grave; the black dog following the descendant home from school each day; the former lapdog living matted and filthy in a dingy alley. Without a regular food source, these Hellhounds survive on their wits and prey on anything with Vitae to prolong their lives. Some are lucky enough to become the companions of other Kindred, but most starve to death or die on the hunt.<br /> <br /> <b>The Companions </b>are the most unsettling breed. These rare creatures have achieved symbiosis with masters who can provide them with the vast quantities of Vitae needed to keep them alive. The sleek, growling shadow at the Lord\u2019s side; the hunting dog with a knack for finding escapees; the massive bear-dog bred to hunt down draugr \u2014 these are pets worthy of a king. Such creatures are nightmare beasts to their masters\u2019 enemies, but remain true so long as their bowl is full of warm, sweet blood. But as their hunger grows, how long can even a powerful Lord keep them fed?<br /> <br /> <b>Rumors</b><br /> <br /> <i>\u201cThe Faithful? Yes, I know of them. My bloodline has raised them for thousands of years. Since the nights of the pharaohs, in fact. They have an uncanny ability to obey our commands and find specific quarry \u2014 ours have been trained to hunt Kindred whose eyes flash yellow in the darkness. Just like your friend there. Good boy.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> The Faithful can be trained to prey on things that aren\u2019t Kindred. An ancient bloodline of Shadows known as the Khaibit bred a lineage of Hellhounds for the specific purpose of hunting down the Strix. This particular line has been all but exterminated through unusual accidents, but a handful survive in Spain, Morocco, and Portugal, where, coincidentally, there are very few sightings of the Owls.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":192,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","user":"Yossarian","date":". 06-10-2021, 06:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T15:11:09-07:00","id":1451584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1451575#post1451575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>The Strays </b>were once proud servants whose loyalty survived the Final Death or torpor of their owners. The dutiful watcher at the master\u2019s grave; the black dog following the descendant home from school each day; the former lapdog living matted and filthy in a dingy alley. Without a regular food source, these Hellhounds survive on their wits and prey on anything with Vitae to prolong their lives. Some are lucky enough to become the companions of other Kindred, but most starve to death or die on the hunt.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This strain in particular makes me remember <i>Tinhoso</i>, a two-headed calf raised to bull in a Gangrel's freakshow until a debacle with (dead) federal agents in the 40s forced him to disband the whole thing in the middle of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantanal\" target=\"_blank\">Pantanal</a>, only to find out much to his surprise 60+ years later that the beast not only managed to survive on its own but eventually tracked him through saving his youngest childe (that it somehow recognized through vitae scent/sympathy it seems).<br /> <br /> Mnemovores i have also toyed with in that same game - connecting them indirectly to the &quot;Second Death/Deep Torpor Realm&quot; from Mythologies, Mnemovores being what results when a diablerist or vampire with the wrong soul is hollowed out by one of the Psychocomps that watch over the domain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 06-10-2021, 06:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T11:22:39-07:00","id":1451704,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">and I think that might be the last excerpt!(?)</div> </div> </div> </div>...is it a Mnemevore joke? Or is it because you got memory eaten by one?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T16:48:11-07:00","id":1451775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1451704#post1451704\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...is it a Mnemevore joke? Or is it because you got memory eaten by one?</div> </div> </div> </div>If only I were that clever. But then, how would I know if my memories had been eaten? Hmmm...<br /> <br /> Basically, I might have some more dev notes if I can think of them, but this'll be the last true excerpt. I may also pop some cut content on the thread though!<br /> <br /> So, the Mnemovores. Originally, they weren't going to be in the book. The author had a concept about memory eating spirits who attack Kindred in torpor, and who <i>created </i>Mnemovores. However, this idea required the use of the ephemeral beings system, which I always find fits into <b>Vampire </b>like a square peg in a round hole (it's partly why the Strix are so simple compared to other ephemera, for example). I also felt like they were just regular spirits with a twist. As I often say, why reinvent the wheel? Mnemovores make more compelling antagonists; they were once Kindred, and therefore, once human.<br /> <br /> The Mnemovores aren't <i>too </i>different from their original form in <b>Wicked Dead</b>. We did fiddle around with the mechanics, but it's a fairly straightforward conversion. Aside from recontextualizing all the stuff about the fog of ages, the big difference is in altering how they spend memories. That was a very late development choice: Basically, spending Skill dots to do things sounds cool on paper, but in practice, it's a bookkeeping nightmare for the ST, and it's not very meaningful for antagonist characters. We'd already changed their feeding mechanics to be more abstract, so they weren't taking dots directly from victims anyway. Instead, I reskinned their fuel-stat as <i>Memoriae</i>, making it more like Vitae points. Mnemovores still lose their Skill dots over time, but the Storyteller isn't forced to constantly readjust dots on the page for the character.<br /> <br /> Here are the Mnemovores. I'll think about what I want to post on Monday, but until then, have a great weekend, and enjoy OPP Con!<br /> <br /> <b>Mnemovores: In Search of Lost Time</b><br /> <br /> <i>Why am I here? Do you know? Please, God, tell me why I\u2019m here! Why do you keep calling me \u201cprince?\u201d'</i><br /> <br /> <b>AKA: </b>Mind Eaters<br /> <br /> Vampires are powerful creatures, with eternal bodies and withered, monstrous souls that (theoretically) last forever. Yet despite their power, their minds remain human, finite, and fragile. Their bodies become predators; their minds remain prey.<br /> <br /> Some Kindred adapt to the times, reinventing themselves as the ages turn and discarding who they once were. Others go into long torpors and wake different, changed in the sleep of ages to prepare for a new world. But some aren\u2019t so lucky. Sometimes the weight of the Requiem breaks a vampire, collapsing her mind under the weight of more memories than the human brain was ever meant to bear.<br /> <br /> From this terrible fracturing, a hungry thing emerges from the wreckage, a being with an insatiable hunger for the lives of others.<br /> <br /> <b>Background</b><br /> <br /> Throughout history, these creatures have been called many names \u2014 Glawackus, Baku, and even Mind Flayers \u2014 but they\u2019re most often referred to as Mnemovores, or just Mind Eaters. They do exactly what that name suggests.<br /> <br /> Where they come from is unclear. Most evidence suggests the Mnemovores are the result of torpors gone wrong, but how this happens is unknown. Most rumors say a vampire\u2019s mind becomes detached from the body if she doesn\u2019t prepare for the sleep of ages well enough. However, a few say the Mnemovores are the result of Dominate abuse, and that when a Lord pushes too hard, or edits a mind one-too-many times, something breaks, destroying the original personality and creating a hungry vacuum.<br /> <br /> Mnemovores store memories like water in a sinking boat, and must expend their minds just to keep going. To this end, like the Kindred they once were, they thirst. However, instead of blood, they hunger for the raw, untouched, and eternal memories of immortal minds, which are far more succulent than the mercurial brains of mortals. In order to feed their needs, the Mnemovores seek out Kindred in daysleep and torpor, and then dismantle them, stripping away their memories until nothing remains but a hollowed-out husk.<br /> <br /> These insidious creatures look like ordinary vampires at a distance, but up close, the differences become horrifically clear. Their skin is papery and dry, their eyes are foggy and dull, and their bodies are frail and hollow. Once fed, these creatures return to a semblance of their former lives and appearances, their minds now muddied and mingled with the stolen memories they\u2019re in the process of digesting.<br /> <br /> <b>The Forgetful Mind</b><br /> <br /> If you haven\u2019t heard of the Mnemovores before, it wouldn\u2019t be surprising. Such stories are rare due to their potent defense mechanism, an aura of amnesia that affects anyone who witnesses their feeding. Only the most resolute can resist forgetting whole encounters with these creatures once they\u2019re out of sight. After all, how can you hunt what you can\u2019t remember?<br /> <br /> The rare few Kindred who escape a Mind Eater\u2019s attack with their memories intact become targets of ridicule, their accounts dismissed out of hand as delusions or lies. However, as technology advances, the Mind Eaters are facing new threats. While even vampiric memory is pliant, those of digital devices are immune to their fog. Their powers of amnesia still affect those viewing the recorded media, but each watch offers another chance to overcome the creature\u2019s influence.<br /> <br /> And once you remember, you\u2019ll never forget again.<br /> <br /> <b>Nightmare Makers</b><br /> <br /> Let it never be said that the Mnemovores only take. Sometimes, when the conditions are right, one of these entities takes a perverse shine or enmity to one of its victims, choosing to seed them with its pilfered memories or fragments of stolen minds.<br /> <br /> And sometimes, when a Mind Eater drinks a bit too greedily or regularly from a Kindred victim, he succumbs, dies, and rises as fresh Mnemovore. However, the Beast is tenacious, and in very rare cases, strong-willed vampires can cling onto the broken pieces of their minds and exist in a torturous and liminal state, not wholly Kindred or Mnemovore, and doubly damned until they can restore their minds.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T13:57:39-07:00","id":1452053,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1451251#post1451251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">PS: Also, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25955-somethingfishy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar25955_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25955\" data-vbnamecard=\"25955\">SomethingFishy</a>'s illustration for the Crassus character might be my favourite one in the book. He embodies the word douchebag.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you gave me a lot of great reference material!<br /> <br /> Actually, after I send my finals in, I realized I had a pdf of Ghouls and could have, like, referenced the old Crassus the entire time. Kinda glad I didn't, but it really is incredible how wealthy business degree douchebag aesthetic has simultaneously come so far and not changed much since that book was published.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Posting gives me anxiety, but I'm still happy to be here</i><br /> Habitual Lurker, Vengrel Liker, Acolyte Apologist, She/Her","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25955-somethingfishy","name":"SomethingFishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25955_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T19:30:57-07:00","id":1452102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SomethingFishy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1449363-false-gods-ventrue-now-available-at-storytellers-vault?p=1452053#post1452053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, you gave me a lot of great reference material!<br /> <br /> Actually, after I send my finals in, I realized I had a pdf of Ghouls and could have, like, referenced the old Crassus the entire time. Kinda glad I didn't, but it really is incredible how wealthy business degree douchebag aesthetic has simultaneously come so far and not changed much since that book was published.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's so funny about the old Crassus image compared to yours. I was looking through <b>Ghouls </b>the other day and thought, &quot;Wow, here's another one we accidentally referenced!&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem